 "kind": "statement",
 "title": "Reduction to a closed subscheme of the target",
 "statement": "Let $i\\colon Y'\\hookrightarrow Y$ be a closed immersion of $X$-schemes and\nsuppose the section $f\\colon U\\to Y$ factors as $f=i\\circ f'$ through $Y'$.\nIf the conclusion of [thm-nagata-conrad-elimination] holds for the data\n$(X,U,V,Y'\\to X,f')$, then it holds for $(X,U,V,p,f)$, with the same\nadmissible blow-up and the same open subscheme of it.",
 "source": {
  "document": "literature/blueprint/master/blueprint/sec-nagata-compactification/sk-thm-nagata-sketch-conrad/lemma-nagata-conrad-closed-target-reduction.md",
  "place": "node lemma-nagata-conrad-closed-target-reduction, order 219"
 },
 "stmt_deps": [
  "def-nagata-admissible-blowup",
  "def-nagata-conrad-quasi-domination",
  "def-nagata-global-blowup",
  "def-nagata-conrad-elimination-conclusion"
 ],
 "proof": [
  {
   "text": "The extension of $f$ is the extension $g\\colon W\\to Y'$ of $f'$ followed by\n$i$; it is a section of $p$ over $W$ and extends $f$ because $i$ is a\nmorphism over $X$. The comparison morphism\n$Y'\\times_XX'\\to Y\\times_XX'$ is a base change of $i$, hence again a closed\nimmersion, and the graph of $i\\circ g$ is the image of the graph of $g$ under\nit. A closed immersion is a closed map, so that image is closed.",
   "uses": [],
   "elementary": null
  }
 ],
